%erg 0.1
Title: Guard against shell loops that poll for a process
Created: 2026-09-10
Author: Minh Ha-Duong

--- log ---
2026-09-10T07:57Z Minh Ha-Duong created
2026-09-10T08:20Z Minh Ha-Duong note Pivoted after review of PR #855: a PreToolUse guard replaces the resident rule, so the budget raise is withdrawn

--- body ---
## Context

An agent that must wait for a background job has no "wait" primitive — the Bash
tool ends when its command ends — so it improvises a shell loop polling `pgrep`
until the job is gone. The loop never ends: the waiting shell's own command line
contains the pattern, so `pgrep` matches it and the loop waits for itself. It
then outlives the session, reparented to init, polling forever.

Measured over 551 session transcripts, 2026-06-01 to 2026-09-10:

- **105 waiters written, every one by an agent.** An independent re-derivation
  during review found 103-122 depending on match strictness, so treat 105 as an
  order of magnitude, not a count. The methodology was never shipped; that is
  itself a defect this ticket does not repeat — the guard's test file carries
  the shapes verbatim instead.
- Several show an agent that had already met the self-match and was working
  around it (`pgrep -c … -le 1`, a double negation). That is the tell that the
  defect is the *shape*, not the predicate.
- **Two stranded processes were alive in 43 h of uptime**, at 14 h 57 and
  16 h 49, both found by accident during an unrelated cleanup.

## Decision

A `PreToolUse(Bash)` guard, `scripts/guard-pgrep-waiter.sh`, in the idiom this
repo already uses for `guard-destructive-bash.sh`: match a dangerous shape in
the command string, deny with a message that names the remedy.

## Why this instrument and not the two that were tried first

**Not a memory note.** One existed from 2026-09-09, with the correct recipe, and
reached none of the 105 — memory is recalled by relevance.

**Not a resident rule.** That was the first attempt (PR #855, first revision).
It cost 474 characters in the system prompt of every session of every project,
which required raising `RESIDENT_BUDGET` from 36 000, which required this ticket
to argue a permanent tax. Review found the argument incomplete: it never weighed
a guard, the cheapest and strongest option, already established in this
codebase. A script costs **zero** resident budget — the cap counts `rules/*.md`
only — and blocks the mistake structurally rather than hoping 474 characters
buried in a 14 kB file are read and recalled.

The same review found the raise's "loan against a trimming pass" framing
unenforceable: nothing in the repo referenced the repayment, and `erg-pr-merge`
closes a ticket unconditionally at merge, so the exit criterion would have
auto-closed unchecked. Withdrawing the raise dissolves that debt rather than
tracking it.

**A `paths:` scope does not apply** and this stays true: it fires on the file
being edited, and this defect happens when a Bash command is written.

## The guard's own cost, measured rather than assumed

A guard reads text, not intent. An earlier draft of this one fired against a
command written to *investigate* these very waiters, in the session that wrote
it. The match is therefore a three-way conjunction — loop keyword, `pgrep`,
`sleep` — chosen so that enumeration (`for pid in $(pgrep …)`), bare
investigation, file polling, and the recommended `kill -0 "$PID"` form all pass.
Every one of those is an explicit allow case in the test.

This is the trade the harness's severity floor asks for: a guard earns its place
against a defect class that demonstrably exists, and 105 instances with two live
strandings is that evidence.

## Relevant files

- `scripts/guard-pgrep-waiter.sh` — the guard
- `tests/test_guard_pgrep_waiter.sh` — six deny cases from real transcripts,
  eight allow cases, and a no-jq case
- `settings.shared.json` + `adapters/claude-code/hooks/hooks.json` — wiring; the
  adapter file is generated, never hand-edited
- `projects/-home-haduong--claude/memory/feedback_pgrep_waiter_matches_itself.md`

## Invariants

- `RESIDENT_BUDGET` is untouched at 36 000, and `rules/workflow.md` is unchanged.
- The adapter hooks stay in sync (`make check-adapter-hooks`).
- No legitimate `pgrep` usage is blocked — the allow arm of the test is the half
  that matters, since a guard is trivially correct if it denies everything.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The guard denies the six measured waiter shapes, including both
      agent-invented workarounds
- [ ] The guard allows enumeration, investigation, file polling and the
      `kill -0` form
- [ ] Wired in `settings.shared.json` with the adapter regenerated and in sync
- [ ] The memory note records the guard as the instrument, and keeps the
      reparentation signal for whoever builds a sweep later
- [ ] `make check` passes
